<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=shaduf">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shadoof</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a> · Also known as shaduf, well sweep, well pole, swape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The shadoof is a tool for lifting water from a well, river or pond onto land or into a channel: a long pole pivoted on an upright frame, with a bucket at one end and a counterweight of clay or stone at the other. The earliest evidence of it is a cylinder seal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> of about 2200 BCE; it appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> sometime after 2000 BCE and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> around the same time. One man can lift 39 to 130 litres a minute with it, and it is still used for irrigation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaduf.jpg" alt="Shadoof" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noumenon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Well-Central_Anatolia.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>One theory holds that the shadoof was invented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> as early as the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, around the 24th and 23rd centuries BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a> are believed to have adopted it next, with evidence of its use from around 2100–1600 BCE. Its appearance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> is placed most likely in the 18th Dynasty. Some historians believe instead that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> invented it along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, citing tomb drawings of shadoofs at Thebes from 1250 BCE. A third theory places its origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at about the same time as in Mesopotamia, because it was so widespread there, but little other evidence supports it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pole is hung on its frame about one-fifth of its length from one end. The vessel on the long arm may be a bucket, a skin bag or a reed basket coated with bitumen, and some have an uneven base or a tie at the top so that the water runs out at once. The operator pushes the vessel down with his own weight, and the counterweight raises it full without effort. The water is emptied into runnels that carry it along irrigation ditches. The frame may be one post or a pair, and the vessel may hang from a rope or from a thinner stick.</p><p>The device lifts water from 1 to 6 metres, and its efficiency has been calculated at 60 per cent. A study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, which measured effort by heart rate, found that suitable adjustments reduced the energy the operator spent from about 109 to 71 watts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> shadoofs set in tiers, one above another, raised water to higher levels by the work of several people. In Egypt under the Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdoms</a>, pleasure gardens watered by shadoof became a mark of luxury residences and a symbol of status. Women have often worked it, since it is easy to use.</p><p>The name comes from the Arabic šādūf; in English it is also called a well sweep or swape. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> knew it as the kēlōn or kēlōneion, the Chinese call it jiégāo, and its Hungarian name, gémeskút, means heron well. Today it is very common in rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On the Great Hungarian Plain it is considered a symbol of the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shadoof” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Aswani wrote that the provisions of the country and its king came from Kersa, the Gezira, where rainfall was enough for rain-fed cultivation, whereas north of the confluence farming was confined to riverside plots watered by the <b>shadoof</b> or the sakia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2200 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mesopotamia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some historians hold that it began on the Nile, citing tomb drawings at Thebes from 1250 BCE; another theory, with little evidence, places its origin in India.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/crane-machine">Crane<span>late 6th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=shaduf">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
